# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them. The =analysis-= notes under =doc/notes= carry the
longer investigations.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* Library

The library itself, =include= and =src=: its behaviour, its interface
and what it carries that it no longer uses.

** TODO An empty =am_pm= fails =%p= where glibc reads AM

=hy_AM=, =sm_WS= and =su_ID= define =am_pm= as two empty strings
and =t_fmt= or =d_t_fmt= with =%r=; =time_put= writes "09:05:09 "
and =time_get='s =%p= now fails against two empty names (before the
parsing-side entry it read 00:00:00 and reported good). glibc's
=strptime= matches the empty AM name and reads 09:05:09; the PM
half has no bytes to carry and cannot round-trip under any parser.

Fix: a decision: match an empty name as AM, as glibc does, or
record the limit.

Check: the round trip's =%X= under the three locales at 09:05:09;
a =22.locale.time.get= =%p= row with empty names either way.

** TODO The wide collate facet compares through the C library by default

=collate_byname<wchar_t>::do_compare= in =src/collate.cpp= goes to
=wcscoll= under =setlocale= unless =_C_use_libstd= is set, while
=collate_byname<char>::do_compare= always transforms through the
library's own database. The asymmetry dates from the 2005 import. A
hypothesis, not a finding: =wcscoll= on the C library's table of the
same name was a shortcut past two transforms, taken when the two
tables were meant to agree; with the tree's databases built from
glibc's sources they mostly do, and where they do not the two facets
of one locale disagree with each other. The collate test and the
oracle set =_C_use_libstd= to reach the database.

Fix: open; either the wide facet takes the database by default as
the narrow one does, or the asymmetry is documented as intended.

Check: =git log -S= for the branch's history, then the 15D suite with
the wide default flipped to the database, against its pin.

** TODO Two pattern-table comments name the wrong pattern

=src/punct.cpp= labels entry 013, ="\4\1\3\2"= {value, space, sign,
symbol}, as ="\4\3\3\2"=; =tests/localization/22.locale.moneypunct.cpp=
labels entry 021 as ="\3\\14\2"=. The data is right in both.

Fix: the two comments.

Check: none; the oracle's copy of the table reads the corrected
comment.

** TODO The numeric conversions of =time_get= read a digit glibc leaves

glibc's =strptime= reads a numeric field only while one more digit
keeps the value within the field's upper bound (=get_number=,
~val * 10 <= to~): =%d= on "45" reads 4 and leaves "5", =%m= on "41"
reads 4 and leaves "1". The library reads the field's digit count
and fails the bound, so =%d= on "45" fails. XSH says only that
leading zeros are permitted but not required. Seen in the probe that
settled the digit counts.

Fix: a decision: follow glibc's early stop, or keep the fixed count
and record the difference.

Check: =22.locale.time.get= rows =%d= on "45" and =%m= on "41",
either way, against a glibc probe.

** TODO The numeric conversions of =time_get= reject leading whitespace

glibc's =strptime= skips white space before a numeric field; the
digit loop of =do_get= does not, and the =22.locale.time.get= rows
for =%d= and =%e= pin that. A hypothesis, not probed: XSH's
=strptime= text decides whether the space belongs to the field.

Fix: open, after reading XSH; either the loop skips white space as
glibc does, or the rows stand as the record.

Check: a glibc probe of =%d= on " 3" beside the =%d= and =%e= rows.

* Tests

The suite and its driver under =tests=: what it covers, what it
misses, and how it runs.

** TODO The suite against other libraries

The suite tests the C++03 library through clauses 18 to 27. Most of
its assertions check standard behaviour, not the library's additions.
Run against libstdc++ and libc++, a disagreement is a defect in one of
the three or a question the standard leaves open.

Fix: build the driver and the tests of clauses 21 to 27 against
another library. The tests that need the library's additions or its
=_RWSTD_= macros are listed and left out, each with the reason.

Check: the census of the tests that build, a table per library, and
each disagreement read against the standard and filed where it
belongs.

** WAITING ThreadSanitizer cannot follow =longjmp= on glibc 2.44

Under the sanitizer every =longjmp= and =siglongjmp= dies with "can't
find longjmp buf" and exit 66. A ten-line program with =sigsetjmp=
and =siglongjmp= and no signal fails the same way, under GCC 16's
libtsan and Clang 23's runtime alike. The sanitizer records the stack
pointer at =setjmp= and, at =longjmp=, demangles the one saved in the
=jmp_buf= to find the record. glibc 2.44 (a5ec880f80, "Consolidate
pointer guard to use a relro variable instead of the TCB") moved the
x86-64 pointer guard from the thread control block slot at =%fs:0x30=
to the variable =__pointer_chk_guard=; the slot now reads zero, and
the runtimes still demangle with it. The same series gave aarch64 a
rotate its runtime does not undo (78f1f0e39c). Reported upstream as
LLVM issue 228263 (2026-10); GCC's libsanitizer takes the fix at its
next merge from compiler-rt. The six programs of the spot-check note
are the ones this stops.

Fix: the sanitizer runtimes'. The tests need no change.

Check: =0.alloc=, =0.new=, =0.inputiter=, =0.outputiter=,
=18.setjmp= and =18.csetjmp= exit 0 under a sanitizer runtime that
reads the new guard.

* Certification

The scripts under =bin= and the nightly run: building the
configurations, running their suites and comparing the tables with
their pins.

* Locales

The locale sources and charmaps under =etc/nls=, the =localedef= that
compiles them and the databases the facets read.

** TODO =localedef= writes no money pattern for an unavailable =sign_posn=

A =sign_posn= of =CHAR_MAX=, unset or written as -1, gives
=format_of= in =util/localedef/wmonetary.cpp= four =CHAR_MAX= parts
in =pos_format= or =neg_format=. That is not a pattern C++ allows
(22.2.6.3 p1); =src/punct.cpp= yields four =none= for the same
fields, not one either. Of the 502 definitions in =SUPPORTED= only
=C.UTF-8= reaches it: its =p_sign_posn= and =n_sign_posn= are -1,
and its unset =int_= ones take them.

Fix: open. Neither the C library nor C++ names a pattern for a value
that is not available; =format_of= and =src/punct.cpp= should agree
on one valid pattern.

Check: the four patterns of =C.UTF-8= and of =punct.cpp='s facets
built from the C locale each hold one =symbol=, =sign= and =value=
and one =space= or =none=.

** TODO Characters outside a collation table sort last, glibc's first

A character that a definition does not name and no =UNDEFINED=
covers gets one shared collation value at every level, after every
defined character. glibc gives such a character =IGNORE= at the
first three levels and a weight at the fourth: =wcsxfrm= of U+743B,
a Han character outside the pinyin table, writes no weight before
the fourth level under =zh_CN.UTF-8=, so it sorts before every
letter. POSIX (XBD 7.3.2.4) says the utility shall warn and place
such characters at the end of the collation order, which =localedef=
has done since the 2005 import. glibc's own comment in
=ld-collate.c= means the same, but its =output_weight= returns 0 for
=UNDEFINED= before writing weights, so a missing character takes
weight index 0, the lowest defined character's, =IGNORE= at three
levels in =iso14651_t1_common=: a defect of 1999 by reading, and
measured on glibc 2.44: under =zh_CN.UTF-8= U+743B transforms to
three empty levels and a fourth-level value, as U+0001 does, and
sorts before it; under =de_DE.UTF-8= the same character gets a
distinct primary weight after every letter, because the common
table gives the Han block computed weights and only the pinyin
table's reorder leaves the rest of the block undefined.
The narrow facet agrees with glibc by accident: its byte lookup
(=src/collate.cpp= near line 400) advances into the character before
it misses, the validity check then starts on a continuation byte and
=do_transform= returns an empty string, so a letter followed by a
missing character sorts before the letter alone; the wide lookup
does not advance and sorts last, as =localedef= intends. The oracles
count 1584 disagreements of 50000 random strings over U+4E00 to
U+9FFF in =zh_CN=, all over characters outside the table; U+061D in
=fa_IR=; U+0D00, U+0D04, U+0D3B and U+0D3C in =ml_IN=; the 9
single-character pairs of 297266 that differ in =en_US=, at the
boundaries of the CJK extension blocks and over the newest symbols.

Fix: POSIX's order and the library's rule, decided 2026-10-28 with
the back-end plan; repair the narrow facet's miss path; record the
oracle's disagreements over missing characters as explained
residue.

Check: the collation oracle over those ranges finds no disagreement
over a character outside the table.

** TODO A character ignored at a level sorts after a weight there

The library and glibc part over strings that differ only in a character
subject to =IGNORE= at some level. Under =sv_SE=, ü (=IGNORE= at the
fourth level) against ÿ (its code point there), equal at the first
three: glibc puts ü first, the library second. Under =en_US=, "é"
against "é>", where =>= is =IGNORE= at the first three levels: glibc
puts the shorter string first, the library second. The same shape
accounts for the 1 disagreement of 50000 random strings in =en_US= and 3
in =fa_IR= and 6 in =ml_IN= once the characters outside the table are
left out. Hypothesis: the fourth level of =iso14651_t1= is
=forward,position=, and for an element subject to =IGNORE= at a position
level the transform writes its =IGNORE= mark, =CHAR_MAX= narrow and
=WCHAR_MAX= wide, which sorts after every weight
(=doc/notes/analysis-collate-transform.md=); the punctuation of the
=<SPECIAL>= section is read backward at the second level, so a string
that mixes it with letters may mix directions as well.

POSIX (XBD 7.3.2.3) decides both pairs: at a =position= level "the
string containing an element not subject to IGNORE after the fewest
collating elements subject to IGNORE from the start of the compare
shall collate first", and a string exhausted of such elements
collates first; at every other level elements subject to IGNORE
behave as if removed (7.3.2.4). glibc's spelling, a count of the
ignored elements before each weighted one and nothing after the
last, realizes that; the library's mark, sorting after every weight,
puts a string with a trailing ignored element after the same string
without it, which the standard forbids. Agreement with glibc on
other strings is no evidence either way.

Fix: a runtime change in =src/collate.cpp=, the count spelling of
=__rw_process_offsets= with the prefix-free rule the weights got in
2026; the standard is the specification and glibc, whose spelling
realizes it clause by clause, a usable oracle for position levels; first
the transform of each pair against =wcsxfrm=, level by level, to see
whether the backward second level adds a second cause. Decided
2026-10-28 with the back-end plan: the writer keeps the zero and the
direction bits.

Check: the collation oracle finds no disagreement over strings of
letters and characters subject to =IGNORE= at the first three
levels in =en_US=, =sv_SE=, =fa_IR= and =ml_IN=.

** TODO =number_value= accepts any integer for the monetary small ints

glibc bounds =sign_posn=, =cs_precedes= and =sep_by_space= and their
=int_= forms to their POSIX ranges and diagnoses the rest; the
front end's =number_value= accepts any integer, and the writer's
=format_of= falls through its default case to four empty parts for
a value it cannot place, as the old compiler did.

Fix: bound the values in =scalar.cpp= as glibc's =ld-monetary.c=
does, with its diagnostic.

Check: a definition with =p_sign_posn 5= draws the diagnostic; the
502 of =SUPPORTED= draw none.

** TODO The sanity test's name patterns fail on the tree's own names

=run_utils -s=, with =RWSTD_LOCALE_ROOT= at the build's =nls= and
=RWSTD_SRC_ROOT= at =etc/nls=, fails 251 of 464 assertions with
either compiler and either =locale=. =locale -a= lists the root
directory, codeset databases such as =ISO-8859-1= among the locales,
and the check wants every name in the =xx_YY= form: 55 fail.
=locale -m= lists =etc/nls/charmaps=, whose files carry glibc's names
without the =.cm= suffix the check greps for: all 196 fail.

Fix, to decide: =locale -a= lists only the locale directories, or
the check skips the codeset databases; the =-m= check takes any file
of the directory as a charmap.

Check: the sanity test in that environment fails no assertion.

** TODO =locale --help= leaves out =-p= and shows the options combined

=print_help= in =util/locale.cpp= lists every option =main= parses
but =-p=. Its synopsis, =locale [-ckhlw] name ...=, shows the
options combined in one argument, but =main= reads one option per
argument, =-ck= the one combination. The reference page,
=doc/stdlibref/localeutility.html=, follows the code.

Fix: add =-p= to the message and spell the synopsis as the page
does.

Check: =locale --help= lists each option =main= parses, and the
sanity test still finds its first line.

** TODO The =localedef= objects have no header dependencies

=etc/config/GNUmakefile.bin= names =localedb.d= as the only
dependency file, so a change to a header under =util/localedef=
leaves the objects that include it stale. Found when a change to
=cmap.h= left =ldef.o= behind and the binary hung in =~cmap_t= on
the ABI mismatch, until the eight objects were removed by hand.

Fix: generate a dependency file per object for the =localedef=
objects as the library's rules do, or list the headers.

Check: touch =util/localedef/cmap.h= and =make localedef= rebuilds
every object that includes it.

** TODO =scan_utf8= rejects the five- and six-byte forms =utf8_of= writes

The charmap's =utf8_of= still writes the five- and six-byte forms of
the original UTF-8 definition, and =scan_utf8=, glibc's decoder,
rejects them, so a lookup by bytes of a character above U+10FFFF
would fail where a lookup by code point succeeds. No charmap in the
tree or on the build machine has such a character.

Fix: decide the ceiling once, 0x10FFFF or the old 0x7FFFFFFF, and
make the two agree.

Check: a charmap with one character above U+10FFFF reads, and the
character is found by name, by code point and by bytes alike.

** TODO =localedef= writes the shared files in place

The shared =LC_CTYPE= file of a charmap and the charmap's codecvt
database are written beside the locale directories by the first
locale to need them: =share_ctype= and =share_codecvt= in
=util/localedef/ldef.cpp= =stat= the file and write it when absent.
Two locales that need one file, built at once, both write. The build
seeds, 52a86884's successor: the first locale of each codeset is
built alone and the others wait on it, which covers the codecvt
database and the ctype file the seed writes. A locale whose
=LC_CTYPE= copies another definition than the seed's, the =am_ET=
and =tr_TR= groups, still writes its ctype file beside a sibling
built at once. Every writer writes the same bytes, so the file is
whole once every writer is done; a reader that opens it between a
truncation and the end of a write sees a short file.

Fix: =write_file= writes to a temporary name in the directory and
renames it over the final name; the rename is atomic and no reader
sees a partial file. The locale's own files take the same path at no
cost.

Check: =make -j16 locales= twice into two directories and =make
locales= once, the three trees byte-identical; a =localedef= run
killed mid-write leaves no file under the final name.

** TODO =etc/nls/posix= is read by nothing

=etc/nls/posix= holds a second, smaller set of definitions and
charmaps (81 and 594 files, =C.src=, =ascii.cm=, Perforce =$Id=
lines) beside =etc/nls/src= and =etc/nls/charmaps=. No makefile,
script, test or document names it; it came with the 2005 import and
was never wired.

Fix: remove the directory, or name what it was for.

Check: =git grep posix/= finds no reference; the build and the suite
are unchanged.

* EDG porting

The tree builds with EDG's =eccp= in strict mode (=-A=) as a
conformance check: a question is settled there before a bug goes to
another compiler. The front end is open source as of 2026-09:
github.com/edgcpp/compiler. The first entry is the build; the rest
are what it found. Each error is read against the standard: a library
defect is fixed here, a compiler defect goes to EDG, and the leniency
of GCC or Clang it exposes is recorded. The =WAITING= entries at the
end are defects reported to EDG or glibc.

A first build on 2026-10-06, at 6bb238b6 with EDG 7.0 over gcc 16 and
glibc, took hand changes to the tree. It configured, built the
library, the test driver and the utilities, and built 257 of 272 test
programs. The suite ran without signals or non-zero exits: 10,049,711
assertions, 16 failed. A second build on 2026-10-08, at 48d6dac1 after
the retirement of =export=, with the same hand changes and the same
front end, gave the same table.

** TODO The =eccp= configuration

=etc/config/eccp.config= is back, restored from before the platform
retirement (dd1128a5) and brought current: =-A -x=, =--no_export=
while the =WAITING= =src/punct.cpp= entry stands, the prelinker
(~PRELINKFLAGS = --prelink_objects~, the step before =ar= in
=GNUmakefile.lib= and =GNUmakefile.rwt=, =*.ti= in the clean rules),
=-lpthread=, and =etc/config/eccp_builtins.h= on =--preinclude_macros=.
The =__EDG__= guards went with the platform retirement (d743fa8d,
12475dfe); comments that name =eccp= survive in =_iosbase.h=,
=_traits.h= and five characterizations, and so do the =eccp= rows of
=etc/config/xfail.txt=.

The default dialect of =eccp= is C++03 (=__cplusplus= is =199711L=).
No dialect option is needed. The C headers are a property of the
=eccp= installation, not of the tree: it searches the resident gcc's
freestanding headers and glibc's, and its macro table carries the
target facts gcc's =float.h= and =limits.h= read.

gcc's =stddef.h= spells =offsetof= as =__builtin_offsetof=, a keyword
EDG enters only when it emulates GNU, Microsoft or Clang. The name is
reserved, so strict mode may provide it; reported as
https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler/discussions/185. =eccp_builtins.h=
maps it onto =__INTADDR__=, EDG's own =offsetof= primitive; the
characterization "a working offsetof() in <stddef.h>" passes with it.
A =-D= cannot carry the definition: =libc_headers.sh= expands =$CPPFLAGS=
unquoted, the shell splits it at its spaces, and every header probe
fails. The file leaves when EDG enters the keyword.

With no feature macro on the command line, =make config= under
=eccp -A= (11S) configures (what differs from gcc's =config.h= is the
entry "The configuration under =eccp= against GCC's"), and
=make lib= then compiles all 98 sources: the
C99 functions the library calls fall back on C90 behind their
characterizations, and the library's own C header facts moved from
=_config-gcc.h= to =_config.h= so that every compiler takes the
wrapper model (the entry "The pure C headers"). The test driver, the
utilities and the tests do not build yet (the entry "The test driver,
the utilities and the tests under =eccp -A=").

Fix: the entries below, in order. The gcc configuration is unchanged:
its =makefile.in= gains an empty =PRELINKFLAGS= and =make -n lib=
shows no prelink step.

Check: an =eccp= build directory configures, builds the library, the
utilities and the tests, and runs the suite. Its table is pinned
beside the six.

** TODO Run-time differences from GCC

Under =eccp -A=:
- =26.valarray.transcend=: nine failed assertions on the =float=
  functions (=std::acos (valarray<float>)= and kin) in the first
  build, where =<cmath>= forwarded its =float= and =long double=
  overloads to the C99 functions glibc withholds from strict C++03.
  The overloads forward to the =double= functions under
  =_RWSTD_NO_C99_MATH= now; the row is to be rerun.
- =18.limits.traps=: =numeric_limits<bool>::traps= is true, as for
  every integer type, but the test's =bool= division by zero did not
  trap. Outside the test, =bool= division by zero traps under =eccp=
  too. The same row fails in an optimized GCC build (12D, 13
  assertions) and once in 12D with Clang: the test counts on undefined
  behavior surviving the optimizer, which is the first suspect.
- =18.support.dynamic=: =bad_alloc::what ()= returns a bad string.
  The first question is whose =bad_alloc= runs, the library's or that
  of EDG's runtime.
- =22.locale.num.put= runs 1,399 assertions against 1,485.

Fix: read each against the standard and the characterization that
decides it.

Check: the rows pass, or the reason is recorded.

** TODO The configuration under =eccp= against GCC's

=config.h= under =eccp -A= differs from GCC's 11S beyond the other
entries of this chapter: no atomic built-ins, no =_RWSTD_THREAD= and
no TLS; =STD_TYPE_INFO= fails (the characterization declares its own
=type_info=; 12475dfe removed the =#pragma define_type_info= that
served =eccp=); =TYPE_INFO_DTOR= and =LIBC_EXCEPTION_SPEC= (#536,
an exception specification incompatible with glibc's) fail; the
special-member characterizations of the exception classes differ.
=STD_NOTHROW= fails to link: =libC.a= defines no =std::nothrow=
object. =long long= and extern templates are absent, which is
correct for C++03.

Without a feature macro glibc shows strict C++03 the ISO names only,
and the POSIX and XSI characterizations fail on an undefined
identifier: =DAYLIGHT=, =TIMEZONE=, =TM_GMTOFF=, =MADVISE=
(=caddr_t=), =THREAD_SAFE_EXCEPTIONS= (=pthread_setconcurrency=),
=VSNPRINTF_RETURN= (=vsnprintf=), =VA_COPY=, =LCONV_INT_FMAT=. The
first build of 2026-10-06 did not see them because its command line
pinned =_XOPEN_SOURCE=. The library does not ask for these names: it
builds without them. Whether the test driver, the utilities and the
tests state a feature level is the entry "The test driver, the
utilities and the tests under =eccp -A=".

Fix: read each difference against the standard and the
characterization.

Check: every difference is correct for C++03 or has an entry.

** TODO The test driver, the utilities and the tests under =eccp -A=

The library builds under =eccp -A= with no feature macro on the
command line. The test driver, the utilities and the tests do not:
without a feature macro glibc shows strict C++03 the ISO names only,
and these programs are POSIX programs, and glibc withholds the C95
wide I/O functions too, which the library's =<cwchar>= re-exports.
The first build of 2026-10-06 did not see this because its command
line pinned =_XOPEN_SOURCE=.

- =tests/src/thread.cpp= (=popen=, =pclose=),
  =tests/src/environ.cpp= (=unsetenv=), =tests/src/process.cpp=
  (=include/ansi/signal.h= under =_RWSTD_NO_DEPRECATED_C_HEADERS=
  stops at line 63), =util/memchk.cpp= (=getpagesize=,
  =mkstemp=).
- Fifteen test programs stop in =include/ansi/cwchar=, lines
  143-157: =using ::fwprintf;= and the other wide I/O functions of
  C95, which glibc declares under =__USE_ISOC95= only. C++03's C
  library is C90 with Amendment 1, so the names are the library's
  to provide; glibc exports them. Dropping them from =std= is not
  conforming; declaring them is right while the symbols exist.
- The programs the first build could not compile either (the entries
  below) are unchanged.

Fix: undecided. The POSIX sources can state their feature level, as
=tests/src/file.cpp= does; the C95 names want either a
characterization that lets =<cwchar>= declare them itself or a
decision that glibc's gap is glibc's.

Check: the driver, the utilities and the tests build under =eccp -A=
with no feature macro on the command line, the suite runs and its
table is pinned, and the GCC and Clang tables do not move.

** TODO The pure C headers

=include/ansi/_c*.h=, RW's own C90 declarations inside =std=, are the
model a strict compiler would want: exactly the C++03 C library,
whatever glibc hides. They are selected by =_RWSTD_USE_PURE_C_HEADERS=
and no configuration has compiled them since the platform retirement.
Two defects stand in the way of a strict build with them:
- =_cstdio.h= declares =vfprintf=, =vprintf= and =vsprintf= with
  =_RWSTD_VA_LIST=, the layout-compatible =__rw_va_list= of
  =VA_LIST.cpp=, and =_cstdarg.h= defines =va_start= and =va_arg= by
  byte arithmetic. On an ABI whose =va_list= is a structure (x86_64,
  aarch64) the compiler's =va_list= converts to neither, so a call
  with a real =va_list= does not compile and the macros compute the
  wrong thing. =eccp= resolves =<stdarg.h>= to its own header first,
  so the library sees the compiler's =va_list=.
- =_cstdlib.h:141= qualifies =qsort= with =_RWSTD_C=, which is empty
  under =_RWSTD_NO_LIBC_IN_STD=; the pure model has no global
  =qsort= at that point. =bsearch= ten lines above calls its C
  function unqualified. Since the library facts moved to =_config.h=
  the macro is undefined for every compiler and the line compiles;
  the inconsistency stays.

Fix: a decision. The pure headers either defer to the compiler's
=<stdarg.h>= for =va_list= and the macros, which makes =<cstdarg>=
and the v-functions of =<cstdio>= wrappers, or they retire.

Check: a build with =_RWSTD_USE_PURE_C_HEADERS= under =eccp -A= and
under gcc, or the headers gone.

** TODO The C++ overloads of the wide string functions under =eccp=

glibc declares the C++ overloads of =wcschr=, =wcspbrk=, =wcsrchr=,
=wcsstr= and =wmemchr= behind =__CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO=, with
=__asm= labels strict mode rejects. =CORRECT_ISO_CPP_WCHAR_H_PROTO=
characterizes whether the compiler accepts them, and =<cwchar>= and
=<wchar.h>= define the macro only when it does. Under =eccp -A= glibc
then declares the C prototypes, =<cwchar>= re-exports them, and
=21.cwchar= fails five assertions on the const overloads, as it did
under Clang before 5569cc60.

Fix: give a compiler without the glibc overloads the const-correct
pair in =std=, the way the Clang repair did.

Check: =21.cwchar= passes under =eccp -A= and its GCC and Clang rows
do not move.

